1. Workflow
Connect to the EC2 server.

Check KOPS cluster and worker nodes.

Create pod.yml.

Create the Pod using kubectl.

Create service.yml with three Service types.

Verify Services and application connectivity.

Push your work to GitHub.

2. Check the cluster

bash
kubectl get nodes
kubectl cluster-info
3. Create pod.yml

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: pod-2
  labels:
    app: swiggy
    env: dev
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app-cont
      image: rajeshrayudu2022/paytm-bus:v1
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80
Create and verify the Pod:


bash
kubectl create -f pod.yml
kubectl get pods
kubectl get pods --show-labels
4. Create service.yml

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: flm
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: swiggy
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: devops
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: swiggy
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30002

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: swiggy
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
Create and verify the Services:


bash
kubectl create -f service.yml
kubectl get svc
kubectl get endpoints
5. Remember the differences
Service

Purpose

Access

ClusterIP

Internal communication

Inside cluster

NodePort

Exposes application through a node port

http://<EC2-node-IP>:30002

LoadBalancer

Requests an external load balancer

External IP/DNS, when provisioned

All three Services select the Pod using app: swiggy.

6. What happens on EC2?
EC2: Provides the virtual machines.

KOPS: Manages the Kubernetes cluster.

Pod: Runs your application container.

Service: Provides a stable way to access the Pod.

AWS Load Balancer: Can provide external access for the LoadBalancer Service.

